What is Multipathing in Linux? 😊 

Multipathing allows a Linux system to access storage through multiple physical paths (cables, HBAs, switches). If one path fails, another takes over—providing redundancy and load balancing.

✅ Why use Multipathing?

High availability

Performance (load balancing)

Path failover

Logical device management

# How to Configure Multipathing in Linux

# Install packages


# RHEL/CentOS
sudo yum install device-mapper-multipath -y

# Ubuntu/Debian
sudo apt install multipath-tools -y
# Enable configuration

sudo mpathconf --enable --with_multipathd y
#Edit config (optional)


sudo vi /etc/multipath.conf
Example:


defaults {
  user_friendly_names yes
  find_multipaths yes
}

# Start service

sudo systemctl start multipathd
sudo systemctl enable multipathd
#Check devices


sudo multipath -ll
sudo lsblk

# Tip: Test by unplugging one path—your system should still access the storage.
